   Typography
   14 Mar 2026 · #showcase #manuscripts #navigation/astrolabe
   ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 ▸ properties · 4

   This note is a specimen sheet: every construct the reader knows how to
   draw, written about instruments, manuscripts and the people who navigated
   by the stars. Read it at different widths to see how the page breathes.

   Text can be bold, italic, both at once, struck through, or highlighted
   like a gilded initial. Inline code such as `altitude = 90° − zenith` sits
   on its own raised ground. A formula stays verbatim: $\tan\theta = h/d$.
   Tags read as chips — #astronomy and #history/islamic — and footnotes as
   small numbered marks.[1]

   Links come in several kinds: a wikilink to Astrolabe, one with a label
   such as the instrument itself, one to a heading in another note like
   Astrolabe › The rete, a link to a Missing note that does not exist yet, a
   Markdown link to the reading list, and an external link to a museum
   catalogue ↗. A bare address is shortened when it is long: example.org… ↗,
   while a short one stays whole: https://example.org ↗.


 ▾ Headings

   Headings build hierarchy from weight and ink rather than size.

